'''basso-di-camera''' (bȧs′ọ̄-dē-kam′e-rä̤), ''n.'' [It.: ''basso'' = E. ''bass''<sup>3</sup>; ''di'', < L. ''de'', of; ''cămera'', < L. ''camera'', chamber: see ''camera''.] A double-bass or contra-basso, reduced in size and power, but not in compass, and thus adapted to small or private rooms. {{smaller|It has four strings, of the same quality as those of the violoncello, but all proportionally thicker.}}

'''basson''' (ba-sôṅ′), ''n.'' The French form of ''bassoon''.—{{smaller|'''Basson quinte''' (kaṅt), a double-reed instrument of which the pitch is one fifth higher than that of a bassoon.}}

'''bassoon''' (ba-sön′), ''n.'' [< F. ''basson'', < It. ''bassone'', a bassoon, aug. of ''basso'', low: see ''base''<sup>1</sup>, ''bass''<sup>3</sup>, ''basso''<sup>1</sup>.] '''1.''' A musical instrument of the

