'''bastardice†''' (bas′ta̤r-dis), ''n.'' [< F. ''bastardise'' (16th century), now ''bâtardise'', < OF. ''bastard'', bastard.] Bastardy. ''Chapman.''

'''bastardise,''' ''v. t.'' See ''bastardize.''

'''bastardism†''' (bas′ta̤r-dizm), ''n.'' [< ''bastard'' + ''-ism.''] Bastardy.

